{"slug":"cli-error-exit-table","title":"Barkpark CLI — Error-code ↔ Exit-code Table (M0 frozen)","blocks":[
{"id":"b1","type":"ingress","content":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the single canonical mapping: the CLI maps the error envelope's "},{"type":"text","value":"code","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":" string — never re-derives an exit code from the HTTP status."}]},
{"id":"b2","type":"callout","tone":"info","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Status:","marks":["strong"]},{"type":"text","value":" DECIDED at M0. This is the single canonical mapping. The CLI maps the error envelope's "},{"type":"text","value":"code","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":" string — "},{"type":"text","value":"never","marks":["strong"]},{"type":"text","value":" re-derives an exit code from the HTTP status. (Contract spine rule #3: "},{"type":"text","value":"one error↔exit table","marks":["strong"]},{"type":"text","value":".)"}]},
{"id":"b3","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"How the mapping works"},
{"id":"b4","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Every coded error from the Barkpark API arrives in the v1 envelope shape:"}]},
{"id":"b5","type":"code","value":"{ \"error\": { \"code\": \"<string>\", \"message\": \"<string>\", \"details\": { … }, \"request_id\": \"…\" } }"},
{"id":"b6","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The CLI reads "},{"type":"text","value":"error.code","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":" and looks it up in the table below to pick its process exit code. The HTTP status is carried only on the response status line and is recorded here for reference — "},{"type":"text","value":"it is NOT the lookup key.","marks":["strong"]},{"type":"text","value":" Two reasons this is load-bearing:"}]},
{"id":"b7","type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[
[{"type":"text","value":"The same HTTP status maps to multiple distinct "},{"type":"text","value":"code","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":"s (409 covers both "},{"type":"text","value":"rev_mismatch","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"text","value":"conflict","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":"; 422 covers "},{"type":"text","value":"validation_failed","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"text","value":"invalid_paper","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"text","value":"invalid_op","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":", and the dynamic patch-engine codes). The exit code distinguishes them; the status cannot."}],
[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"text","value":"code","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":" is a stable contract string; a status can drift (e.g. a 412 added later for "},{"type":"text","value":"precondition_failed","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":"). Keying on "},{"type":"text","value":"code","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the CLI stable."}]
]},
{"id":"b8","type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"text","value":"error.code","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":" is absent or unknown (see \"Codes with no "},{"type":"text","value":"error.code","marks":["code"]},{"type":"text","value":"\" below), the CLI falls back to "},{"type":"text","value":"exit 1","marks":["strong"]},{"type":"text","value":" (generic/unexpected)."}]},
{"id":"b9","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"The stable exit-code scheme"},
{"id":"b10","type":"callout","tone":"info","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Codes 0–5 are byte-identical in meaning to the published CLI handbook; 6–8 are purely ADDITIVE (the handbook did not enumerate conflict / rate-limit / server). No handbook code meaning is redefined.","marks":["strong"]}]},
